{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfkde2fxpRN5yqjBWQMrG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WpacJ9CopDeD2tCYim6Eun","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_5\nSRC=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/models/reversed_mapping_second_order_gamblers.py\necho person_length_scale_reversed_gamblers > $D/model_name.txt\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nRefinement of `reversed_mapping_second_order_gamblers` (people judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, gambler's-fallacy model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators they suspect — a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, a repeating motif with slips, with their own motif suspicion and switch belief — with a side habit and a person-specific share of trials answered the wrong way round). The single change is that people differ in how they weigh evidence against sequence length: each person has their own length scaling, drawn from a population, so some judge the total evidence (long sequences feel decisive, two- and three-flip pairs barely matter) while others judge the evidence per flip (short pairs feel as clear-cut as long ones). This addresses the critique that people prefer the more-switching sequence in very short pairs less than one shared length scaling predicts, by letting that preference vary between people rather than across the population as a whole.\nEOF\n/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python - << EOF\nsrc=open(\"$SRC\").read()\nold='''    gamma = pm.Normal(\"gamma\", mu=0.5, sigma=0.5)\n    trial_len = seq_len[idx_a]\n    length_scale = pt.exp(-gamma * pt.log(trial_len / 5.0))\n'''\nnew='''    # Person-specific (non-centred population, 400 slots): each person weighs\n    # evidence against length with their own power gamma_i.\n    mu_gamma = pm.Normal(\"mu_gamma\", mu=0.5, sigma=0.5)\n    sigma_gamma = pm.HalfNormal(\"sigma_gamma\", sigma=0.5)\n    z_gamma = pm.Normal(\"z_gamma\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0, shape=400)\n    gamma_i = (mu_gamma + sigma_gamma * z_gamma)[participant_id]\n    trial_len = seq_len[idx_a]\n    length_scale = pt.exp(-gamma_i * pt.log(trial_len / 5.0))\n'''\nassert old in src\nsrc=src.replace(old,new)\ni=src.index('\"\"\"',3)+3\ndoc='''\"\"\"Person-specific length scaling of the evidence on the reversed-mapping gambler's second-order judgment.\n\nRefinement of `reversed_mapping_second_order_gamblers` (described below). Single\nchange: the power gamma with which the evidence is divided by sequence length,\n(n / 5) ** -gamma, is person-specific, gamma_i = mu_gamma + sigma_gamma * z_i\n(non-centred, 400 slots), instead of shared: some people weigh total evidence\n(gamma_i ~ 0; short pairs barely matter), others per-flip evidence (gamma_i ~ 1;\nshort pairs as decisive as long ones). Addresses the critique that in length-2/3\npairs people prefer the more-switching sequence less than one shared length\nscaling predicts.\n\nDescription of the refined model follows.\n\n'''\nsrc=doc+src[3:]\nopen(\"$D/candidate.py\",\"w\").write(src)\nEOF\nhead -30 $D/candidate.py; cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $D --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1642,"cache_read_input_tokens":94996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1642,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_ge